then I tend to think that...

1) If the goal is to fund ExI, sure, but if it is to cover costs...

2) It seems to me the list run on fairly elaborate software in terms
of filtering, subscriptions and whatnots, and is not very elaborate
in terms of distribution system (one machine forwards the mail to
everybody). The result is, of course, a high computation, storage, and
networking load on the machine.

A solution could be:

A) establish a list with no gimmicks: basic subscription mechanisms,
a la listserv, and that's it, no filters. You can of course keep a list
with all the trimmings for pay. The same email address at extropy can
relay to both.

B) distribute the free list via a cooperative fashion: extropy sends
only to a few people who receive the list through their own filters
anyway (newsreaders, procmail, deliver...). These people's filters can
automatically forward each message they receive to several other
people with negligible load.

I could do that on my account with negligible work. What this scheme 
requires is a way for new subscribers to distribute themselves under
maybe 2 levels of remailers, no big deal I would expect.

