From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extortion Countermeasures in Crypto-Anarchy

Handling Extortion in Crypto-Anarchy


Charlie Stross, David Friedman, Hal Finney, Nick Szabo, Derek Zahn,
Sasha Chislenko, and others have commented on extortion,
murder-for-hire, and other "black" aspects of crypto-anarchy. (Recall
that I mentioned these explicitly in my 1988 "Crypto-Anarchist
Manifesto," e.g., my mention of "odious markets.)

There are many interesting issues, some of which have been resolved,
others of which are largely open questions. 

This posting deal with a couple of issues:

1. Comments on Nick's ideas and comments.

2. A brief analysis of extortion and countermeasures.

(Sorry for combining these into one posts, but I started on #1 and got
carried away in a digression into #2. I'm writing this in an on-line
editor, so it's not convenient for me to split this into two parts.)

Here's the #1 part.

I like Nick Szabo's "Crypto Shops of Isher" idea. I read "Weapon
Shops" back in the mid-60s and had forgotten most of the details, but
I do remember thinking at the time that *of course* free availability
to weapons is a Good Thing, whatever the risks.

(And, yes, crypto has risks. All private communication and private
behavior makes law enforcement harder. Crimes can be planned, carried
out, etc. Stolen goods can be hidden, and on and on. Blackmail,
extortion, and poison pen letters all flowered after the introduction
of private mail delivery, where the return address was incorrect or
missing. I mention these points very briefly to note that crypto
merely extends to certain electronic communications many of the same
things we already have around us, plus a few new twists.)

This is the #2 part, on dealing with extortion in crypto-anarchy.

I welcome the interest being shown here by some Extropians in crypto,
crypto-anarchy, BlackNet-type things, and so on. My main problem is
that I don't have a lot of patience--some would say I have _no_
patience--in covering old and familiar ground. Robin Hanson, Dean
Tribble, and I debated the "extortion" problem a while back, on this
List I believe (Robin wasn't on the Cypherpunks list, so it was likely
here), and we reached some conclusions:

* extortion happens when "benefits to extorter" > some threshold
(which may be zero)

* benefits to extorter = (chance of getting a payoff from victim) *
(size of payoff) - (direct costs) - (chance of getting caught) * (cost
if caught, in punishment, restitution, etc.)

* you can plug in some numbers to see if you want to become an
extortionist, arsonist, or a murderer-for-hire.

* with anonymous payments, the chance of getting caught can be made
quite low...this is the main factor that leads people to believe
extortion will be rampant.

(BTW, the famous Chaumian "bank can collude" case can be solved by the
extorter specifying the bank, which may actually be his bank or one he
"trusts" not to collude, in the same reputation way he trusts his
Swiss bank today not to arbitrarily zero out his account, or he can
specify multiple banks, multiple transfers. And since he can afford to
wait until the deposit "clears," he can use many options, most of
which are nearly impossible to trace.)

* Thus, extortion, murder for hire, arson, etc. is expected to
flourish. Digital protection money, so to speak.

* Except that the victim or target of the extortion *also* has an
equation to think about:

- he pays only when the (costs of payments) < (benefits)

- importantly, he may be so *flooded* with spurious (or even real)
threats that he has no way of knowing which are serious (some
interesting measure-countermeasure stratgies exist, such as bombarding
oneself with anonymous threats so as to appear to be drowning in a sea
of threats...the real extortionist is told to "get in line.").

* Benefits of paying = a complicated function of whether the extortion
threat is real, whether the threat will in fact be carried out if the
extortee doesn't pay, the chance the extorter will be caught in the
act, etc. (You can try to make up your own equation for this, to
understand how the terms contribute, but it'll be too messy to talk
about it here).

* In a nutshell, suppose a crypto-extorter, Elvis, sends a note
demanding that Vic, his victim, pay him some amount of crypto-money.

Vic has to decide what the threat is. Will Elvis really follow
through? Will Vic's existing defense systems be sufficient? Etc.

The "untraceable" payment is only a small part of this issue. For
example, suppose Elvis sends an ordinary mail message to the Vic Tuna
Company, announcing that unless Vic stops using drift nets, its
corporate headquarters will be blown up. (I suspect such anonymous
extortion demands are fairly common, at many levels of governments,
President on down). The threat is anonymous, the "payment" is also
untraceable (though Greenpeace may be a _suspect_). 

Or the President receives an anonymous threat commanding him to take
some action. The threat is anonymous, the "payment" is untraceable.

Has the world ended? If not, why not?

* Because various stratagems have evolved for dealing with extortion
demands and ransom notes. "We don't negotiate" is a classic (honored
in the breach at times). "Crank mail" dismissals (even if real, the
threat is taken seriously...real extortionists know that most threats
to the President will be thusly dismissed, unless a "rep" is
provided). Or low-level official screen all such messages, so
extortionists face a gauntlet to get their messages through.

(These evolved strategems will be used and adapted for the
crypto-anarchy regime.)

* Because the cost-benefit analysis makes some threats credible,
others not. Real extortionists are careful to establish credibility,
to reduce the "doubt discount" which would normally be applied, I'll
leave it as an exercise to think about how such "reps" are established
and maintained.

* Imagine a pure crypto-anarchy world of anonymous messages,
untraceable payments, etc. Several trends are likely:

1. After an initial period of confusion, some people would send
extortion messages out to many people (if one victim is good, a
thousand are better....best of all is the entire community).

2. This "flooding" will make any one threat less scary, less likely to
be acted upon.

3. "Real" extortionists will have to make some "examples" of victims
who naturally chose not to pay. Digital signatures, time-released
announcements, etc. will establish the "rep" of the extortionist.

4. However, the real world actions--arson, murder, etc.--will have
some cost, either in the costs of hiring the arsonists, in the chances
of getting caught, or in the revealing of valuable blackmail
information, or the like. (That is, there's a "cost of doing business"
than enters into the equation I described early on.)

(Parallels with the IRS are obvious. Many threats, a few "examples
made." But a new threatening agency, an extortionist, will have a very
hard time achieving the same "credibility" the IRS has acquired.)

5. And the "examples" may not reach everybody. Some people will tune
out the news, will be unaware of the dangers, and will not
"understand" how to make the system work. Playing dumb may work.

There are many, many other issues, and the game theoretic aspects need
a lot more work. 

And disconnecting one's pseudonyms from one's "true name" remains an
important strategy.

